WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.598
[NOISE]
Well

00:00:04.598 --> 00:00:10.648
Jack, we're

00:00:10.648 --> 00:00:16.194
delighted to

00:00:16.194 --> 00:00:22.479
see you here.

00:00:22.479 --> 00:00:25.990
You said you'd never write another book,
what made you write this one?

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.699
&gt;&gt; Well, I went around the world for the
last three and

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:32.670
a half years, talking to audiences like
this and listening to questions.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.170
The first book was an autobiography.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:34.930
It was all about me.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:39.260
And this book is trying to codify some
things

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.120
that I learned in business, in response to
these questions.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.350
So this book, hopefully, is about you.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.733
And hopefully about audiences and some of

00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:50.253
the things that people have been
challenged

00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:54.700
by in picking a company to work for,
working for a bad boss, or anything.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.890
&gt;&gt; The book's called Winning, so what is
Winning anyway?

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:04.740
&gt;&gt; I think Winning is without question
about defining what your objectives are.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.580
Clearly laying them out, and then going
for

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.640
it with everything you've got, and getting
there.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:15.160
&gt;&gt; So one of the things in the book that
you're quite outspoken about is, candor.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:21.430
You say let me tell you about the biggest
dirty little secret in business.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:24.268
That in every culture, in every country,
in every

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:28.120
society, and social class there's this
lack of candor.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:33.460
Why is that so important, why is it so
hard, how do you change that?

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.496
&gt;&gt; I think you have to build an
organization, it's quite

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:40.390
good in a small start-up, where people are
all comfortable with each

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.218
other, they know what the mission is, they
understand the values,

00:01:44.218 --> 00:01:48.260
they reward the values, and they go after
it with a passion.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.800
In a bureaucracy, it tends to get more and
more subjective.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.360
The pressure is from the side, from the
top, from underneath.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.410
People are afraid to speak out.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.908
And what it does, it slows you down.

00:01:59.908 --> 00:02:05.220
It, really puts gum in the years.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.010
And it really doesn't improve the
workplace.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.975
People, I'm being told what they're doing

00:02:12.975 --> 00:02:15.863
well and how they can improve the
evaluation

00:02:15.863 --> 00:02:22.400
process is not frequent enough we get into
this I'm too kind to evaluate my team.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.327
This morning I was in San Jose with about
500 executives from

00:02:26.327 --> 00:02:31.146
start up companies down there and some
pretty strong tech companies Intel and

00:02:31.146 --> 00:02:35.296
others, and I asked the 400 people how
many people thought they

00:02:35.296 --> 00:02:38.634
had straightforward relationships in their
company

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:42.440
with their peers and with their
associates.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.020
I didn't get four hands.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.510
Four hands, that's frightening.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.650
It's sort of frightening that people are
sitting in organization and don't

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.820
feel that people are laying it on straight
and telling it like it.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:56.920
What's your experience?

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.900
&gt;&gt; I was lucky to work for a good

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:03.290
company where over time we really
developed this honest feedback.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.130
But it wasnt that way when I first joined.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:06.250
&gt;&gt; It takes real time.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.825
&gt;&gt; it takes real leadership and
commitment, yeah.

00:03:07.825 --> 00:03:10.900
&gt;&gt; And [UNKNOWN] reward that value.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.770
In the end, you get the behaviors you
reward.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:21.280
If you reward candor, if you reward
straightforward talk, you will get it.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.200
If you, on the other hand, don't do that.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.349
You'll get close to the best behavior.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.840
&gt;&gt; You've also been outspoken, not only on
candor, but on differentiation.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.660
That part of candor's to be honest with
people about where they stand.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:43.580
And, you're noted for this top 20, middle
70, bottom 10,

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:45.035
&gt;&gt; Noted is a kind word.

00:03:45.035 --> 00:03:47.343
[LAUGH] [NOISE].

00:03:47.343 --> 00:03:50.580
&gt;&gt; Tell us a little bit more about
differentiation, and,

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.082
shouldn't we be a little tougher on
student grading, perhaps?

00:03:54.082 --> 00:03:57.489
[NOISE].

00:03:57.489 --> 00:04:03.430
&gt;&gt; Well I think you, I think you probably
get a differentiating school.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:05.286
And you recognize who the top students

00:04:05.286 --> 00:04:07.490
are, and you recognize where the middle
is.

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:08.810
And you recognize the bottom.

00:04:08.810 --> 00:04:15.977
The thing that's crazy is, why is grading
and differentiation okay in the

00:04:15.977 --> 00:04:23.503
fourth grade through getting an MBA, but
it in no way is applicable to adults.

00:04:23.503 --> 00:04:25.000
It's nuts.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.238
Why you would end up having this false
kindness, where you

00:04:29.238 --> 00:04:33.920
don't tell people where they stand until
you run into trouble.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.292
So on my view is, take care of the top 20

00:04:37.292 --> 00:04:41.130
and this isn't a permanent thing, it
changes all the time.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.310
But take that top 20, make 'em feel loved.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:49.380
How you give them cash, give him rewards
in the soul, in the wallet.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.020
Do everything for him.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.240
That middle 70, show them what they need
to do to get in the top

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.780
20, and that bottom ten, tell them not

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.090
that, that why they basically should move
on.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:04.860
And don't do it in a guillotine job.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.610
Have a conversation that goes over a year
or so about what their shortfalls are.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.460
Tell them they're in the bottom ten.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.880
Don't give em a raise of any kind.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.338
Don't give them 2 or 3%, that fake raise
that keeps people hanging around.

00:05:17.338 --> 00:05:23.690
Cut off the, the salary issue and then ask
him to leave.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:25.850
And, let's say, over the next several
months,

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.720
work together to get you in the right
place.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.480
That's so much better.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.660
In these crazy situations, companies in,
in the Valley here, they run into trouble.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:36.365
What'll they do?

00:05:36.365 --> 00:05:37.290
They gotta cut costs.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:39.980
They, they gonna have a layoff.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:44.086
They walk into people and they say, look,
Joe, Mary, we've

00:05:44.086 --> 00:05:47.840
got to cut you back, we've got to take you
out.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:48.850
We need to cut costs now.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.440
And they say why us?

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.720
And they say, but you weren't that good.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:58.310
And then they say, but we've been here 12
years and nobody ever told us that.

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:01.440
That's what happens in this false kindness
thing.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.760
People get misled.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.680
And then, if you do it too late in your
career, I

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.200
maintain that not having the
differentiation

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.640
is the cruelest form of manager.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:12.580
The cruelest thing.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.210
If you have the responsibility, if you

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:17.640
lead people, they should know where they
stand.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.951
As you go out from school here now, with
shiny MBAs,

00:06:20.951 --> 00:06:25.340
when you go work with somebody, initially
it's gonna be about you.

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:28.010
You'll be a leader.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:30.595
You'll be an integral contributor, staring
in something

00:06:30.595 --> 00:06:33.130
and starting something up or doing
something else.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:34.960
But it'll be about you.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.776
The day you become a leader, managing

00:06:37.776 --> 00:06:41.650
five, eight, ten people, it becomes about
them.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.650
And your future is tied to them.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.650
You no longer do the nitty gritty little
stuff.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.450
You build them into great people.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.666
And you get a kick out of that, and then

00:06:55.666 --> 00:06:59.060
you get the benefits of their success as
you go up.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:04.496
If you keep doing what you started doing
when you joined the place, doing your job,

00:07:04.496 --> 00:07:09.950
your project rather than building your
team, you go nowhere in a layered society.

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:15.867
&gt;&gt; In the book, you use candor and
differentiation

00:07:15.867 --> 00:07:19.012
and a few other things, mission and values
and voice

00:07:19.012 --> 00:07:22.373
and dignity as what you call first
principles, you just

00:07:22.373 --> 00:07:25.189
got to have this in a company to be
successful.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:31.250
Are you surprised at how few organizations
really have these first principles?

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:31.750
&gt;&gt; Shocked.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:36.140
I mean it is, mission and values are

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:40.870
the most gobbley gooked conversation
pieces in companies.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:42.990
I mean, a mission statement ought to be so
clear.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:45.380
It ought to exactly know where you're
gonna go.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.350
Define it clearly and go after it.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.895
Not have this mission of goodness and all
of

00:07:49.895 --> 00:07:53.160
these other words that get in our mission
statements.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.630
Make it very clear what you want to be.

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:58.780
And then have the values, which I call
behaviors.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:01.890
Values in missions, values are behaviors,
the

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:05.450
behaviors you want to achieve that
mission.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:07.816
And then you measure and reward as I

00:08:07.816 --> 00:08:11.330
said in the sole and the wallet, those
behaviors.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.655
And when people don't exhibit those
behaviors and you want them

00:08:15.655 --> 00:08:20.300
and you finally have to part company with
people because they didn't

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:24.705
exhibit the behaviors and values you
wanted, you can't say that

00:08:24.705 --> 00:08:30.310
they went home to spend more time,
personal time with their family.

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:32.920
You gotta say here's why these people
didn't make it.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:37.230
They're good people, they're this and that
but they didn't have our values.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:41.740
One of the craziest things you see in
corporate America, it's run

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:46.870
by lawyers in some ways, where, where you
end up with integrity violations.

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:50.970
And people say, well, we had to let so and
so go.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.600
They want to spend more time at home.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:09:00.800
You got to say, you got to hang them
publicly for doing bad stuff.

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.050
You've got to set the tone of your values
in your behavior, and

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:08.860
if you're doing it right, you can't be
afraid to put it out there.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:11.380
And so I think that you set a mission, you
set

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:16.600
the behaviors, you operate in an open
candidate, you said it

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:21.810
takes time, build trust, transparent
fashion and you give every employee

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:24.539
voice and dignity and you've got a
foundation that means something.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:30.358
&gt;&gt; You mentioned trust and I know in your
section, in your chapter

00:09:30.358 --> 00:09:34.590
on leadership and one of the key things
leaders have to earn trust.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:38.100
Nobody gives it to you, you've gotta go
out and earn it.

00:09:39.332 --> 00:09:41.329
Do you, I'd like to ask you a

00:09:41.329 --> 00:09:45.840
question today with compensation and
benefits and stuff.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.009
Do you think it's possible that executives
can be paid so much

00:09:50.009 --> 00:09:54.350
that it's really hard for them to win the
trust of their people?

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:59.390
If they don't think it's right or fair?

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:04.290
&gt;&gt; We, we have to lay out clearly what the
game is.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.539
I mean, we have a free market system.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.600
We have capitalism.

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:10.650
It has its flaws.

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.121
There's no way to come to a perfect answer

00:10:13.121 --> 00:10:17.040
to every single, what the absolute salary
ought to be.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.990
But it ought to be pretty transparent.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:21.980
People ought to know what the
opportunities are, and, and

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:24.430
the opportunities to grow on, and, and to
get it.

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:28.660
And I don't think salary into a, into a
lack of trust issue.

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:31.602
I think if you, now if you got a jerk boss
getting too

00:10:31.602 --> 00:10:35.710
much my money and not building a team, of
course you'll have it.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:40.990
But if you're building a team together
around a common vision, you alone have it.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:44.420
But the whole issue of salary and
severance, and

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:49.080
all these things that are big popular
subjects today.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:53.720
And, severance in particular is, is one
that drives everyone crazy.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:58.250
And, the problem with severance is, we
just one with Callie PP

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:03.133
Arena here in this area, where she got $22
million for leaving.

00:11:03.133 --> 00:11:06.780
The problem is not Callie with her 22
million.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:10.957
The problem is the board at Hewlett
Packard that

00:11:10.957 --> 00:11:16.250
did not, did not have a succession plan in
place.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:22.075
So that when the existing CEO failed, and
they threw him out, they

00:11:22.075 --> 00:11:28.940
had to go out and get somebody, and they
had to offer them the world!

00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:29.640
To get them.

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.770
If they had that succession plan that
wouldn't have, most

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:36.230
of these problems come at the front end of
these deals.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:39.000
They, they get written up and beat up on
the

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.520
back end, but the problem came on, the
front end.

00:11:44.026 --> 00:11:47.319
Tyco for example, you probably all know
about Tyco

00:11:47.319 --> 00:11:50.240
and Krovloski and all the mess that they
had.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.370
They didn't have a succession plan.

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:57.110
So they went to Motorola to get the number
two person there, Ed Breen.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:58.554
Now Ed Breen is not gonna go to

00:11:58.554 --> 00:12:02.150
this tarnished place that's a disaster
without a package.

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:09.340
Well they had backup [UNKNOWN] up, to get,
to get Ed Breen to come there.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:14.000
Ed Breen had had all the negotiating
tools, because, there was nothing there.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.712
When, when we had our success session at
GE, we had three contenders at the end:

00:12:18.712 --> 00:12:23.790
Jeff ML, Bob Nardelli, who went to Home
Depot, and Jim McNerney who went to 3M.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:25.260
All very capable.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:27.640
All equally capable in, in many, many
ways.

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:31.210
Jeff got the job.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:33.735
Bob goes to Home Depot.

00:12:33.735 --> 00:12:35.709
They didn't have a succession plan.

00:12:35.709 --> 00:12:37.492
Jim goes to 3M.

00:12:37.492 --> 00:12:40.184
They didn't have a succession plan.

00:12:40.184 --> 00:12:43.217
Jim and Bob, Jeff got a raise.

00:12:43.217 --> 00:12:44.360
Nice going, Jeff.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:45.855
Congratulations, you got a raise.

00:12:45.855 --> 00:12:50.975
[LAUGH] Jim and Bob, the trucks were
loading the money on the back of the van.

00:12:50.975 --> 00:12:56.800
[LAUGH] Cuz those two companies didn't
have succession plans.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:02.537
So my argument is, succession plans are
the answer to controlling these outrageous

00:13:02.537 --> 00:13:07.485
severance and these other [INAUDIBLE]
where you don't have these deals.

00:13:07.485 --> 00:13:09.320
&gt;&gt; Bob was here earlier in the year for a
view

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:13.767
from the top, and he looked like he was
doing well.

00:13:13.767 --> 00:13:15.500
[LAUGH]

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:18.380
&gt;&gt; You say something in the book that,
didn't

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:20.450
shock me, but it may shock a lot of
people.

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:24.005
And that is, you said, you know, the chief
human resources officer

00:13:24.005 --> 00:13:28.670
is at least, if not more important, than
the chief financial officer.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:33.663
&gt;&gt; And I'll tell you, we're not even close
to what we have to be in this area.

00:13:33.663 --> 00:13:37.189
[COUGH] most unfortunately, I was in, I
was in Mexico giving a

00:13:37.189 --> 00:13:41.050
talk with my wife Suzie, who, by the way,
wrote this book.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:44.190
So I, I'm just a mouthpiece in this game.

00:13:46.010 --> 00:13:51.160
We were in Mexico for 5,000, 5,000 HR
people were, were there.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:56.880
We asked, I asked a question to the
audience.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:00.770
Raise your hand if you're perceived by the
organization to

00:14:00.770 --> 00:14:03.430
have a seat at the table equal to the CFO.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:05.950
I didn't get 50 hands.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:10.341
Now if I asked that question at GE, there

00:14:10.341 --> 00:14:14.220
isn't one HR person that wouldn't raise
their hand.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.710
Every one of them knows they're equal to

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:19.390
or more important than somebody counting
the numbers.

00:14:20.758 --> 00:14:25.113
I always liked to use the, the analogy if
you were coaching a baseball team or a

00:14:25.113 --> 00:14:27.860
football team, would you wanna hang around
with

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:30.878
a team accountant, or the head of player
personnel?

00:14:30.878 --> 00:14:32.910
If you wanted to build a great team.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:36.815
The accountant can't do a damn thing for
you, except tell you how

00:14:36.815 --> 00:14:41.110
much money you got left to offer to Barry
Bonds, or anybody else.

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:42.840
But he can't do much else.

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:48.160
The idea that CEOs hang around with the
CFO, the grunt.

00:14:48.160 --> 00:14:54.270
The guy with the green eye-shades is
crazy.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.864
The HR person is the person that if, if

00:14:56.864 --> 00:15:01.353
they're not the, most companies may make
them the, uh-.

